\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We identify conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO) as a systematic failure mode of temporal conditioning in imagination-based actor-critic training. The core finding---that trained $\dt$-embeddings with multi-rate data exposure reliably induce value overestimation while frozen embeddings never do---reveals that the failure lies in co-adaptation between the temporal embedding and the world model, not in the conditioning architecture itself. The NVE diagnostic provides a practical detection tool with perfect classification across our experimental conditions.

The four-level intervention hierarchy yields two insights for the broader model-based RL community. First, gradient magnitude does not predict causal importance: the dominant gradient pathway (observation decoder) is causally inert, while the weakest (reward head) drives CIVO---a cautionary result for gradient-based attribution in complex architectures. Second, dual critics converge when trained on shared imagined rollouts, rendering Double Q pessimism structurally ineffective; this constraint applies to any imagination-based system with shared features, not only DreamerV3.

For practitioners extending world-model RL with temporal or other auxiliary conditioning signals, we recommend: (1)~validate with frozen-embedding controls before deploying learned embeddings; (2)~monitor NVE throughout training as an early warning; (3)~prefer MPC-style re-planning over pure imagination when variable-rate execution is required.

Future work should examine whether CIVO manifests in transformer-based world models, diffusion-based planners, and MPC architectures with extended rollout horizons. The wide dual-critic result---where min-aggregation plus increased capacity achieves partial prevention through an as-yet-unidentified mechanism---suggests that architectural solutions exist but require deeper investigation into how capacity interacts with value estimation under temporal conditioning. Code released upon acceptance.

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

All experiments use DreamerV3's RSSM; generalization to transformer-based world models \citep{micheli2023iris}, TD-MPC variants \citep{hansen2024tdmpc2}, or diffusion-based planners is untested. The evaluation covers two locomotion and one manipulation environment with proprioceptive observations---pixel-based or real-robot settings remain open. Seed counts (4--5; \citealt{colas2018seeds}) provide sufficient statistical power for our binary classification but may miss subtler effects.

